  "exact_identity": "M* base = base(zeta2)/beta  (trivial: M*=zeta2/(2 zeta1), base(zeta1)=base(V)=beta)",
  "why_no_universal_law": "base(zeta2)=7*growth_base(sum_P m_P^2 * 14^{-weight(P)}) is an INDEPENDENT spectral quantity of the state, not a function of beta. Spread spectra (Haar-pure, noisy, low-rank) give 7; concentrated stabilizer spectra (product, GHZ) give 15/2; ceiling 34/4=17/2=8.5. Both pure Haar (base 7) and pure product (base 7.5) exist, so purity alone does not fix it. Hence no f(beta) reproduces base(zeta2) across states.",
  "conjecture_internal_flaw": "It correctly uses FULL V for beta but implicitly assumes DIAGONAL-only (7^n) for zeta2. The zeta2 off-diagonal (state Z-string weights m_P^2) lifts concentrated states to 15/2 by the same mechanism the prompt flagged for V.",
  "paper_impact": "NONE. The paper asserts base 28/5 only for its noisy-pure q=0.1 ensemble (base_z2=7, beta=5/4); it does not claim a universal 7/beta law. No paper.tex claim is contradicted."
